  (text "GD2\nRRO op-amp, +5V\ngain ~1.5 (3.3V DAC -> ~5V gate)\ne.g. TI LM7321 (RRO)" (at 143 63 0) (effects (font (size 1.1 1.1)) (justify left)))
  (text "R2 10k\nsafe-default\npull-up on\nGD2 INPUT" (at 123 55 0) (effects (font (size 1.0 1.0)) (justify left)))
  (text "R3\n10R\ngate" (at 167 70 0) (effects (font (size 1.0 1.0)) (justify left)))
  (text "Q2\nlow-Rds logic-level N-ch\n~3mohm e.g. TW100N03CC\nFULL-ON for brushless\nIRFP250N = brushed-only" (at 191 64 0) (effects (font (size 1.0 1.0)) (justify left)))
  (text "D2\nSS310\ncatch\n100V" (at 204 68 0) (effects (font (size 1.0 1.0)) (justify left)))
  (text "RESISTANCE-CONTROLLED BRAKE (linear, no PWM):\nDAC1 (0-3.3V) -> GD2 (non-inverting, gain ~1.5) -> ~5V max -> Q2 gate.\nQ2 is operated in its LINEAR region: gate voltage sets Rds,\nwhich is placed across the motor (low-side, TRACK_POS to RED).\nMode B indexes one of seven stored brake tables\n(25/15/5/4/3/2/1 ohm full scale); Mode A linear-maps the pot.\nNo switching, no PWM, no switching noise." (at 15 100 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.1 1.1)) (justify left)))
  (text "SAFE DEFAULT — CRITICAL:\nR2 (10k) is on the GD2 INPUT pin, NOT on Q2 gate.\nWhen the MCU is unpowered/reset, the GD2 input is pulled to a\nsafe-default level so Q2 defaults to braking. If R2 were on the\ngate, the buffer would fight it." (at 15 125 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.1 1.1)) (justify left)))
  (text "Q2 BRAKE FET — LOW-Rds, brushless-dominant (80%):\nBrushless braking shorts the Remora ESC bus; motor 31-273mohm is\nBELOW the FET Rds, so the brake is FET-limited. Q2 must be single-\ndigit-mohm logic-level (e.g. TW100N03CC), driven FULL-ON. IRFP250N\n(75mohm) gives only 9-78% brake + ~120W at 40A => brushed-only.\nBrushed linear tables: low-Rds FET VALIDATED in linear - brushed op\npoint is low Vds (~8V on a 30V part), clear of the high-Vds linear-\ninstability region. One FET serves both. See design doc 7A.12." (at 15 145 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.0 1.0)) (justify left)))
  (text "D2 SS310 CATCH DIODE — ESSENTIAL:\nMotor inductance produces a large voltage spike on current\ninterruption. SS310 (100V) chosen for headroom on the 16V rail.\nClamps the spike to Vf + Vdc." (at 15 168 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.0 1.0)) (justify left)))
)
